meta {
  name: Upload New Business Application
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

put {
  url: {{nbaDocumentUploadUrl}}
  body: text
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/pdf
}

body:text {
  %PDF-1.4
  1 0 obj<</Type/Catalog>>endobj
  trailer<</Root 1 0 R>>
  %%EOF
}

tests {
  test("storage accepts the application", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
}

docs {
  # PUT <uploadUrl> — the raw new business application upload (PAC-56 #23)

  Not an API endpoint: this goes **straight to object storage** using the URL
  from `Presign New Business Application`.

  - **Auth:** `none` — deliberately. The collection-level bearer would be sent
    on this request and **break the S3 signature**; a presigned URL carries its
    own credentials.

  ## Headers
  `Content-Type` must be `application/pdf` — it has to match the signed value,
  and this kind accepts nothing else.

  ## Body
  A minimal valid PDF stub. The bytes are irrelevant to the API; only the
  object's type and size matter, and `POST /sold-deals` reads both back with
  `HeadObject` rather than trusting anything declared.

  ## Run order
  ⚠ Runs **after** `Create Sold Deal` (seq 4) because of Bruno's numeric
  ordering, so `nbaDocumentKey` is not what that request uses — it reuses
  `soldDocumentKey`, which is already a PDF under the shared prefix and is
  therefore valid for a discount proof.

  These two requests exist to pin the **presign contract** for the application
  kind: that it is PDF-only and that it lands under `/nba/`. The full
  presign → upload → declare chain for the application, including the
  cross-kind bypass attempt, is covered by the e2e suite
  (`new business application (PAC-56 #23)`), where key prefixes can be asserted
  directly without depending on request ordering.
}
